Discord replaced 177 Cassandra database nodes with 72 ScyllaDB nodes. Uber was handling millions of queries per second using Cassandra.

Ask most engineers why Discord moved away from Cassandra while Uber kept scaling it, and they'll say "Cassandra doesn't scale". That's not it.

Discord had trillions of messages stored across 177 nodes. When thousands of users opened the same popular channel, their requests could overwhelm the same database partition.

So Discord built an intermediate service in Rust that combined identical requests into a single database query.

Instead of repeatedly fetching the same row for thousands of users, the service could fetch it once and share the result.

They also migrated to ScyllaDB, reducing the cluster from 177 nodes to 72. Historical message read latency at p99 dropped from 40-125 ms to around 15 ms.

Uber took a different approach. Its engineers invested in automated repairs, detecting inefficient queries, and tooling to manage Cassandra at a much larger scale.

You learn these trade-offs from engineers who actually operated these systems, not simplified interview examples.

